# Open goals: instruments the completeness critic named as missing. No verified quote yet, so no lemma; add one to axioms/ with an exact quote to prove it.
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-INDIAN"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Indian Reorganization Act, Act of June 18, 1934, ch. 576, sec. 2, 48 Stat. 984 (25 U.S.C. 5102, formerly 462): existing periods of trust and restrictions on alienation extended and continued until otherwise directed by Congress", "likely_url": "https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title25-section5102&num=0&edition=prelim", "why": "This is a general theorem for the trust-extension orders. One statute extends every trust period indefinitely, so every later trust-extension EO becomes unnecessary and every earlier one is overtaken. None of the candidates covers it."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-INDIAN", "PD-LAND-WITHDRAWAL"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Act of March 3, 1927, ch. 299, 44 Stat. 1347, secs. 1 and 4 (25 U.S.C. 398a, 398d): oil and gas leasing on Executive-order Indian reservations, and changes in the boundaries of reservations created by Executive order only by Act of Congress", "likely_url": "https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title25-section398d&num=0&edition=prelim", "why": "It freezes the boundaries of every EO Indian reservation and settles by statute the question in the 34 Op. A.G. 171 candidate. That makes it the definitive instrument for the class."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-INDIAN", "PD-LAND-WITHDRAWAL"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Act of June 30, 1919, ch. 4, sec. 27, 41 Stat. 3, 34 (43 U.S.C. 150), and Act of May 25, 1918, 40 Stat. 570 (Arizona and New Mexico): no Indian reservation to be created or enlarged by Executive order except by Act of Congress", "likely_url": "https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title43-section150&num=0&edition=prelim", "why": "This ends the class of Executive-order Indian reservation creations and gives every such order a closing date."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-NATIONAL-FOREST"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Forest reserve statutes: Organic Administration Act of June 4, 1897, 30 Stat. 34, 36 (16 U.S.C. 473, power to modify or revoke reserves); Transfer Act of Feb. 1, 1905, 33 Stat. 628 (administration to Agriculture); Act of March 4, 1907, 34 Stat. 1256, 1269, 1271 (forest reserves renamed national forests; no new reserves in six named states except by Act of Congress); Act of June 25, 1910 and FLPMA sec. 704 are already in the candidates", "likely_url": "https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title16-section471&num=0&edition=prelim", "why": "The 708 national forest orders have no axiom in the candidate set. Taken together, these acts transfer the whole class, rename it, close it in six states, and supply the authority under which later orders modified or abolished reserves."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-MILITARY-RESERVATION", "PD-LAND-WITHDRAWAL"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Engle Act, Pub. L. 85-337, 72 Stat. 27 (Feb. 28, 1958), 43 U.S.C. 155-158: withdrawals of more than 5,000 acres for defense purposes only by Act of Congress", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-72/pdf/STATUTE-72-Pg27.pdf", "why": "It ends Executive-order military reservations above the acreage threshold. No candidate addresses the 316 military-reservation orders."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-MILITARY-RESERVATION"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Act of July 5, 1884, ch. 214, 23 Stat. 103: disposal of abandoned and useless military reservations, by transfer to the Interior Department on the President's declaration", "likely_url": "https://www.loc.gov/law/help/statutes-at-large/48th-congress/session-1/c48s1ch214.pdf", "why": "This is the general instrument that returns the military-reservation class to the public domain. Many of the orders are declarations made under it."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-WAR-EMERGENCY", "PD-ADMIN"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Overman Act, Act of May 20, 1918, ch. 78, sec. 6, 40 Stat. 556; and First War Powers Act, 1941, Title I and sec. 401, 55 Stat. 838: redistributions of functions by Executive order expire six months after the war ends, and functions revert", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-55/pdf/STATUTE-55-Pg838.pdf", "why": "These are self-executing sunset clauses for every war reorganization order issued under them. Combined with the 1921 resolution and Pub. L. 80-239 they date the end of the whole class. The candidates list the termination dates but not the provisions that make the orders lapse."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-WAR-EMERGENCY"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Proclamation 4417 (Feb. 19, 1976), 'An American Promise', 41 FR 7741, 90 Stat. 3067: confirms that Executive Order 9066 terminated on the issuance of Proclamation 2714 (Dec. 31, 1946)", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-90/pdf/STATUTE-90-Pg3067.pdf", "why": "This is the definitive termination evidence for EO 9066 and the orders issued under it. Without it, Hirabayashi and Endo are left as the only lifecycle evidence for those orders."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-WAR-EMERGENCY"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Ludecke v. Watkins, 335 U.S. 160 (1948): a state of war is not ended by the cessation of hostilities", "likely_url": "https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep335/usrep335160/usrep335160.pdf", "why": "This is the court holding that stops anyone from treating Proclamation 2714 as the end date of orders contingent on the war. It guards the whole WWII class against a false 1946 end date."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-WAR-EMERGENCY"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Emergency Powers Continuation Act, Pub. L. 82-450, 66 Stat. 330 (July 3, 1952), and the joint resolutions that followed in 1952-1953 (e.g. Pub. L. 82-366; Pub. L. 83-5, 67 Stat. 18) extending listed provisions", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-66/pdf/STATUTE-66-Pg330.pdf", "why": "Pub. L. 82-313 continued the listed provisions only to June 1, 1952. Without these later extensions, orders resting on those provisions would get end dates that are too early."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-CLASS-EVENTS", "PD-MILITARY-RESERVATION"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Treaty of General Relations between the United States and the Republic of the Philippines, July 4, 1946, Art. I, 61 Stat. 1174 (TS 1568)", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-61/pdf/STATUTE-61-Pt4-Pg1174.pdf", "why": "This is the bilateral instrument that withdraws U.S. sovereignty and keeps the reserved bases. It is stronger than the proclamation alone for the Philippine reservation orders."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-CLASS-EVENTS"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Panama Canal Treaty of 1977, Art. II and XI (TIAS 10030, 33 UST 39): the treaty terminates the 1903 convention and the Canal Zone ceases to exist on Oct. 1, 1979", "likely_url": "https://www.loc.gov/item/2018697123/", "why": "This is the root instrument that ends the Canal Zone orders. The 1979 Act only implements it. My likely_url is a guess; check the Treaties and Other International Acts Series or state.gov for the official copy."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-CUSTOMS-CONSULAR"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Reorganization Plan No. 1 of 1965, 79 Stat. 1317, 30 FR 7035: abolishes the offices of collector of customs, comptroller of customs, surveyor of customs and appraiser of merchandise, and transfers their functions to the Secretary of the Treasury", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-79/pdf/STATUTE-79-Pg1317.pdf", "why": "This is a class-ending instrument for the customs district and port orders, which name these offices. The 263-order customs and consular family has no candidate axiom."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-CUSTOMS-CONSULAR"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Rogers Act, Act of May 24, 1924, ch. 182, 43 Stat. 140, and Foreign Service Act of 1946, 60 Stat. 999 (with its repeal section): consolidation of the diplomatic and consular services", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-60/pdf/STATUTE-60-Pg999.pdf", "why": "These supersede the statutory base of the consular-regulation and consular-service orders as a class."}
{"added_on": "2026-11-06", "families": ["PD-REFUGE-LIGHTHOUSE"], "found_by": "formulario-axiom-sweep critic", "instrument": "Reorganization Plan No. II of 1939, sec. 2 (Bureau of Lighthouses consolidated into the Coast Guard) and sec. 4(f); Reorganization Plan No. III of 1940, sec. 3 (Fish and Wildlife Service); National Wildlife Refuge System Administration Act of 1966, Pub. L. 89-669, sec. 4, 80 Stat. 926, 927 (16 U.S.C. 668dd) consolidating refuges into one system", "likely_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-80/pdf/STATUTE-80-Pg926.pdf", "why": "Together these transfer and then confirm the lighthouse reservation and wildlife refuge classes (243 orders). The 1966 Act consolidates every refuge established by Executive order into the System. The candidate for Plan II states its scope without the lighthouse transfer."}
